{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And if despite these.</b> The plagues." "<b>Seven.</b> Because it is a complete number, it is said in plural form. So too 'for the righteous falls seven times and rises' (Proverbs 24:16), and 'she who was barren has borne seven' (1 Samuel 2:5). And 'upon your sins to add seven' is not to add seven, but only 'and I will add once more,' like 'a great voice, and it did not add' [i.e., and did not continue]." "The meaning of <b>upon your sins</b> is: because of your sins. And one says [the explanation is] that the number is literal. But the first is correct, for if we count: 'and I broke for you the staff of your bread' (one), 'your sky like iron' (two), 'your land like copper' (three), 'spent in vain' (four), 'the yield of the land' (five), 'and the fruit of the tree' (six), 'and the beasts of the field' (seven)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {2 "The enumeration does not fully map to Leviticus 26:18-22 clause by clause; translated hyper-literally as Ibn Ezra's compressed shorthand without expanding his logic."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:12:44.844030505Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}